                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



  Sofía had a notebook out. She looked alive. “I need all of you in the
same room for the next six months.”
  “Welcome to the table,” said Mama Tete, dryly. “Eat your flan.”



It was Pri who showed us the app.
   She hadn’t said much. She slid her phone across the table, face-up,
and said, to the plate: “Everybody at my school has this.”
   The icon was a leaf. Soft, rounded, a gentle green. Calm Block. Free,
no ads. Sleep. Focus. Feel better. Under that, in small gray type, a com‐
munity project of Aperture Civic. The install count was a number I read
three times: 60K+ in the Bronx.
   I tapped it.
   Rain. A brook. A hush of leaves. And under them, in the same place
every eight seconds, a pressed place the width of a hand.
   Mama Tete’s whole face went still. Yenni’s hand went to her mouth.
Rafa shut his eyes. Desmond, who’d been on his fourth plate, put down
his spoon.
   And Sofía, who can’t hear a note, who has never claimed to, who
said an hour ago that she believed in speakers and ledgers and nothing
else, set down her fork and said, in a faintly puzzled voice:
   “I don’t hear anything. Why do I want to lie down?”
   “Turn it off,” said my mother.
   She was standing in the kitchen doorway with a dish towel in her
hand. She hadn’t said a word during the whole meal. She was looking
at the phone the way she’d looked at the stone in my backpack, with
her face very still and her hand flat against her chest.
   Pri tapped the screen, and the rain went away.
   The room came back. Sound by sound, a refrigerator, a chair, Des‐
mond’s breath. Nobody spoke for a while.
   “Sixty thousand,” said Sofía finally, quietly, to nobody.
   In a borough of a million and a half, that was one phone in every
twenty-five, every one of them playing rain.




